To **remove a specific value from an array in core JavaScript**, you can use the [`Array.prototype.filter`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/filter) method or [`Array.prototype.splice`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/splice) in combination with [`Array.prototype.indexOf`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/indexOf).

Here are the most common approaches:

---

### 1. Remove **all** occurrences of a value (returns a new array)

```js
const array = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4];
const valueToRemove = 2;

const result = array.filter(item => item !== valueToRemove);
console.log(result); // [1, 3, 4]
```

---

### 2. Remove **first** occurrence of a value (modifies the original array)

```js
const array = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4];
const valueToRemove = 2;

const index = array.indexOf(valueToRemove);
if (index > -1) {
  array.splice(index, 1);
}
console.log(array); // [1, 3, 2, 4]
```

---

### 3. Remove **all** occurrences (in-place, modifies original array)

```js
let array = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4];
const valueToRemove = 2;

